  "query_preview": "..\nOverall, the main change driving this forecast package is the \ndesignation of Invest 91W by the JTWC and its subsequent upgrade to \n\"Low\" status. Currently, guidance does not develop 91W into a \ntropical cyclone until well west of the Marianas. During its \ndisorganized stage, it likely will bring heavy rainfall to Guam over \nthe next couple of days. Numerous locally heavy showers and isolated \nthunderstorms are expected for Guam Tuesday through Tuesday night, \nwith high-end (50%) scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms for \nthe CNMI. Model guidance ranges from 2 to 5 inches of rain across \nGuam through Wednesday evening and 1 to 3 inches of rain across the \nCNMI. See the Hydrology section for more details. \n\nAs Invest 91W lifts off to the northwest, the monsoon trough looks \nto lift north of the region, shifting the active wet phase of the \nmonsoon over the Marianas. See Tropical Weather Discussion for more\ndetails. Currently, scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms \nthrough the period are forecasted. Shower coverage could increase \nacross the region late this week and into the weekend depending on \nthe setup monsoon trough. If Invest 91W tracks southerly, it could \nkeep the monsoon trough further south over the Marianas. This would \nput under a col where island effect showers would be possible, \nsimilar to what we saw this past weekend. For now, the focus is heavy\nrainfall on Tuesday and Wednesday, but if the Marianas see \nadditional rainfall during the first half of the week, flooding \nconcerns would increase heading into the weekend. &&",
